It's thoroughly obvious that there are a large number of players who
are sympathetic to the balance changes you are making.  It's obvious
your work has an audience.  There are always players who want
something to be harder, meaner, with fewer loopholes, more aggressive
towards the player, and so on.  For them the fun is the challenge of
defeating a relentlessly aggressive enemy.  But that is not, however,
what everyone wants. You are conflating "many people like it" and
"everyone likes it."

> Which changes do you object to?

That's not really material.  The point I was trying to make, and which
I am pretty sure I did make, is that if you make "fewer loopholes" an
option rather than a monolithic feature, the result will have a wider
audience.  But, as has been pointed out many times in many ways for
many years, many (etc.) players feel that simply having the *option*
to play an "easier" game cheapens the game and  provides an
opportunity to dismiss the different approaches that other players
prefer.  "You played the game with the 'easy' button pressed, so your
lame ascension doesn't count."  Mandatory orthodoxy is not what a
*game* needs.  There's enough of that in the world already.

Allowing people to accept challenges is not the same as requiring
people to accept them.

Anyway, that's pretty clear, and I have nothing else to say on the
matter that hasn't already been said.  In fact, everything that I just
said has already been said.



